{"version":"ai-mcq-bank-v2-la-batch15-high-quality-rewritten-affected-2026-05-22-stem-fixed-inequality-rendering-fixed-student-facing-qc-2026-05-31-pt-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch2-foundations-counting-2026-06-11-pt-batch3-counting-probability-rules-2026-06-11-pt-batch4-conditioning-2026-06-11-pt-batch5-random-variables-2026-06-11-pt-batch6-discrete-distributions-2026-06-11-pt-batch7-distribution-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch8-continuous-expectation-2026-06-11-pt-batch9-non-week7-expectation-transform-2026-06-11-pt-batch10-non-week7-process-summary-2026-06-11-pt-batch11-non-week7-final-summary-shape-2026-06-11-quality-round1-2026-06-14-quality-round2-2026-06-14-quality-round3-2026-06-14-quality-round4-deep-2026-06-14+quality-round5-difficulty-2026-06-14+quality-round6-expression-2026-06-14+quality-round7-answer-cue-2026-06-14+quality-round8-answer-shape-2026-06-14+quality-round9-answer-position-2026-06-14+quality-round10-template-padding-2026-06-14+quality-round11-generic-stems-2026-06-14+quality-round12-deterministic-cleanup-2026-06-15+quality-pilot-deep-rework-2026-06-15","concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Math-II-Linear-Algebra/eigenvalue-and-eigenvector.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1b-Math-II-Linear-Algebra/eigenvalue-and-eigenvector.html","questions":[{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What equation links an eigenvector $p$ and its eigenvalue $\\lambda$?","options":["$A+\\lambda I=p$","$Ap=p+\\lambda$","$pA=\\lambda A$","$Ap=\\lambda p$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0eb113b732351ea88f6bddee"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the zero vector not allowed to be an eigenvector?","options":["Because $A0$ is undefined.","$A0=\\lambda0$ holds for every $\\lambda$, giving no direction.","Because zero vectors exist only over complex numbers.","Because eigenvalues must be zero."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4226ba2a72a69b0e93d1ec7e"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does an eigenvector represent geometrically?","options":["A vector whose length must stay unchanged","A vector always sent to zero","A direction preserved by the transformation","A vector perpendicular to every column of $A$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8f51096e33495401043277e2"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the eigenvalue measure along an eigenvector direction?","options":["The determinant of every submatrix","The scaling factor","The number of columns in $A$","The dimension of the matrix"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e462fb6bc2f817ce27c351ca"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_005","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a diagonal matrix, where can its eigenvalues be read from?","options":["From the last column only","From the sum of all entries only","From its diagonal entries","From its off-diagonal entries"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8d070f22ad89ae55989ade03"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_006","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Eigenvalues are obtained as the roots of which polynomial?","options":["the characteristic polynomial $\\det(A-\\lambda I)$","the minimal spanning polynomial","the determinant of $A$ alone","the trace polynomial"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c2684231212314534a743635"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_007","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $A=\\operatorname{diag}(2,3)$, is $p=(1,1)$ an eigenvector?","options":["Yes, with eigenvalue $2$, because the first diagonal entry equals $2$","No, because $Ap=(2,3)$ is not a scalar multiple of $(1,1)$.","Yes, with eigenvalue $5$.","Yes, with eigenvalue $3$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"94fc80cdbb056885574fb2aa"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_008","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $A=2I_2$, which nonzero vectors are eigenvectors?","options":["Only $(1,0)$","Every nonzero vector in $\\mathbb R^2$","No nonzero vector","Only $(0,1)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"12c2ac9ae403d72977313d29"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What are the eigenvalues of a triangular matrix?","options":["its diagonal entries","its off-diagonal entries","its row sums","always $0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"73e52e3ff2b6edd496697e3d"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_010","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\0&5\\end{pmatrix}$ and $p=(1,0)$, which statement is correct?","options":["$p$ is not an eigenvector because the eigenvalue is zero.","$p$ is an eigenvector with eigenvalue $0$.","$p$ is the zero vector.","$p$ is an eigenvector with eigenvalue $5$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2948eda69055e349a61aa6c6"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $Av=0$ for some nonzero vector $v$, what does that say about $A$?","options":["$A$ has no eigenvalues.","$v$ is not an eigenvector because the output is zero.","$0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$.","$1$ is an eigenvalue of $A$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f005f590598e6a74a4e36e0b"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $p$ is an eigenvector for $\\lambda$, what about $cp$ for $c\\neq0$?","options":["$cp$ is never an eigenvector.","$cp$ is also an eigenvector for the same $\\lambda$.","$cp$ is an eigenvector only when $c=1$.","$cp$ has eigenvalue $c\\lambda$ automatically."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b05582ceee7c517b3a8debbe"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_013","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the eigenspace for an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ built from?","options":["All vectors in the codomain regardless of $A$","The solutions of $Ap=p+\\lambda$","The nonzero solutions of $(A-\\lambda I)p=0$, plus zero","Only the diagonal entries of $A$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"aaffe76517bc6fdaefb3de56"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}$, which vector is an eigenvector with eigenvalue $1$?","options":["$(1,-1)$","$(1,1)$","$(1,0)$","$(0,1)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1ec5ac4d7dcb2f4f4ab62ad5"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, which statement is true?","options":["Both vectors have eigenvalue $-1$.","Neither vector is an eigenvector","Both vectors have eigenvalue $1$.","$(1,1)$ has eigenvalue $1$; $(1,-1)$ has $-1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2602f69d12f882ec009c10c9"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a square matrix, the product of all its eigenvalues (with multiplicity) equals:","options":["$\\det(A)$","$\\operatorname{tr}(A)$","$0$","$1$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1fe02c7eb9079c0cee4debef"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Does a repeated eigenvalue automatically provide several independent eigenvectors?","options":["Yes, the number of independent eigenvectors always equals the repetition count.","No. Repeated eigenvalues may have too few independent eigenvectors.","Yes, but only for nonsquare matrices.","No, repeated eigenvalues cannot have eigenvectors."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d04eabb660cd03c582fe5750"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Eigenvectors corresponding to distinct eigenvalues are:","options":["linearly independent","always equal","always orthogonal by definition","linearly dependent"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b25f4c6ef4638cc66b76a35c"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For $A=\\begin{pmatrix}3&1\\\\0&3\\end{pmatrix}$, which vectors are eigenvectors for $\\lambda=3$?","options":["Only the zero vector","All nonzero vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$","Nonzero multiples of $(0,1)$","Nonzero multiples of $(1,0)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c129efc7f92755d9076f5ed5"},{"id":"la_batch17_eigenvalue_and_eigenvector_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Over the real numbers, what happens for $A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$?","options":["It has no real eigenvectors.","Every nonzero real vector is an eigenvector.","It has eigenvalue $0$ with eigenvector $(0,1)$.","It has eigenvalue $1$ with eigenvector $(1,0)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b855f7bff908a6ab5a0ae9ac"}]}}